  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 307 PM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: High pressure will remain in control of our \nweather across the north country through early Saturday morning.\nTonight will be another cool one with mainly clear skies and \nsome fog formation in the valleys, some patchy frost in the \ncolder spots of the Adirondacks also. Increasing clouds on \nFriday and into Friday night from the southeast will help to \nprevent both fog and frost formation Friday night. Low pressure \ncurrently located off the coast of the Carolinas will track \nnortheastward, and bring heavy rain and strong winds to parts of\nthe northeast. Our region will be a bit on the periphery of \nthis system, but will still have some gusty winds Friday into \nSaturday, and some rain showers Saturday into Saturday night. \nThere is still some uncertainty with respect to how far north \nand west the showers spread into our region. Because of this, \nthere's some uncertainty with how much precipitation we have. At\nthis time looks like we could have about half an inch of \nprecipitation in Windsor county, and the international border \ncould be dry. We will have even less rain in northern New York, \nmaybe just a couple tenths of an inch in parts of Essex County, \nNew York. Models are also indicating that this Nor'easter is \nlikely to have bands of precipitation associated with it, which \nmakes it a bit difficult to forecast the precipitation amounts \nand location of the heavier rainfall. There is a strong jet \nassociated with this system, which will lead to some gusty \nsurface winds. The higher terrain of Vermont and northern New \nYork as well as areas around Lake Champlain will have the \nstrongest wind gusts. Temperatures will be on the cooler side as\nwell, especially Sunday as clouds and periods of showers are \nexpected, especially across southern and eastern Vermont. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Any lingering showers from the Nor'easter should\ntaper off on Monday, though some clouds will likely linger. A \nBermuda High will shift northwest and attempt to gain influence \nover the region for mid-week, but a cold front will be dropping \nsouthwest out of Canada. While the cold front will likely \nprevent the warm airmass from being completely established, \ntemperatures will likely rise into the 70s and dew points should\nrise well into the 50s for much of the region. There will be a \nfew showers associated with the front, but no consistent \nrainfall. However, ensemble guidance completely diverges after \nthe mid-week, with solutions split between the Bermuda High \nbuilding back and a second cold front pushing through. \nTherefore, the forecast is a blend between the two solutions and\nit will likely split one way or the other.",
